1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,771
For about four and a half billion years, the same grey rock has circled the Earth.

2
00:00:05,271 --> 00:00:07,260
It pulls the oceans up and down.

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:10,369
It sets the clock for animals all over the planet.

4
00:00:10,369 --> 00:00:13,415
It even helps steady the tilt of the whole world.

5
00:00:13,915 --> 00:00:16,536
So what if, tonight, the Moon just disappeared?

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:21,951
First, the rules.

7
00:00:21,951 --> 00:00:24,050
At midnight, the Moon vanishes.

8
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,165
No explosion, and no rocks raining down on us.

9
00:00:27,165 --> 00:00:29,873
It's simply gone, and so is its gravity.

10
00:00:30,373 --> 00:00:32,122
Everything else stays the same.

11
00:00:32,122 --> 00:00:35,281
Earth keeps circling the Sun, and the Sun keeps shining.

12
00:00:35,781 --> 00:00:37,436
And here's what we're losing.

13
00:00:37,436 --> 00:00:41,943
The Moon is about three thousand four hundred and seventy-five kilometers wide,

14
00:00:41,943 --> 00:00:44,054
roughly a quarter the width of Earth.

15
00:00:44,554 --> 00:00:48,774
It sits about three hundred and eighty-four thousand kilometers away.

16
00:00:48,774 --> 00:00:52,872
Its light takes about one and a quarter seconds to reach your eyes.

17
00:00:55,633 --> 00:00:59,132
If it happens near a new moon, you might not notice at all.

18
00:00:59,132 --> 00:01:03,401
On those nights, the Moon is up during the day, lost in the Sun's glare.

19
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:06,317
But on a full moon night, it's instant.

20
00:01:06,317 --> 00:01:09,723
Moonlight is just sunlight bouncing off that grey rock.

21
00:01:09,723 --> 00:01:12,386
Switch it off, and the landscape goes dark.

22
00:01:12,386 --> 00:01:15,916
Your moon shadow disappears, and far more stars come out.

23
00:01:16,416 --> 00:01:17,573
Here's a surprise.

24
00:01:17,573 --> 00:01:19,887
The Moon isn't actually very bright.

25
00:01:19,887 --> 00:01:24,193
It reflects only about twelve percent of the sunlight that hits it.

26
00:01:24,193 --> 00:01:25,735
It's a dark, dusty grey.

27
00:01:25,735 --> 00:01:29,527
It only looks brilliant because the sky behind it is black.

28
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:33,152
Something else changes that nobody could feel.

29
00:01:33,152 --> 00:01:36,179
Earth and the Moon don't simply orbit one another.

30
00:01:36,179 --> 00:01:38,601
They both circle a shared balance point,

31
00:01:38,601 --> 00:01:43,143
about four thousand six hundred and seventy kilometers from Earth's center.

32
00:01:43,643 --> 00:01:46,182
That's inside the Earth, but well off-center.

33
00:01:46,182 --> 00:01:49,060
So every month, our whole planet wobbles around it.

34
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,412
At midnight, the wobble stops.

35
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:53,821
Earth carries on around the Sun, alone.

36
00:01:56,567 --> 00:01:58,835
By morning, sailors and surfers notice.

37
00:01:58,835 --> 00:02:01,337
The tide doesn't come in the way it should.

38
00:02:01,837 --> 00:02:05,916
The Moon's gravity pulls hardest on the side of Earth facing it,

39
00:02:05,916 --> 00:02:07,701
and weakest on the far side.

40
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:10,378
That stretches the oceans into two bulges,

41
00:02:10,378 --> 00:02:14,840
and as Earth spins through them, most coasts get two high tides a day.

42
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,999
The Sun raises tides too.

43
00:02:16,999 --> 00:02:21,580
But its pull on the oceans is less than half as strong as the Moon's.

44
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:23,173
About forty-six percent.

45
00:02:23,673 --> 00:02:25,407
So now the Sun is in charge.

46
00:02:25,407 --> 00:02:30,051
Tides don't disappear, but they shrink to less than half of what they were.

47
00:02:30,051 --> 00:02:34,758
By our rough math, about a third the height of today's biggest spring tides.

48
00:02:35,258 --> 00:02:37,845
And those spring and neap tides are gone.

49
00:02:37,845 --> 00:02:41,443
That monthly rhythm came from the Sun and Moon lining up,

50
00:02:41,443 --> 00:02:43,462
then pulling against each other.

51
00:02:43,462 --> 00:02:47,060
With one partner gone, the tides lose their monthly beat.

52
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,491
In Canada's Bay of Fundy,

53
00:02:49,491 --> 00:02:53,563
the sea can rise up to sixteen meters between low and high tide.

54
00:02:53,563 --> 00:02:56,490
That's about as tall as a five-story building.

55
00:02:56,990 --> 00:03:01,131
Without the Moon, those famous tides would be a shadow of themselves.

56
00:03:01,131 --> 00:03:04,852
Tidal power stations, which run on the rising and falling sea,

57
00:03:04,852 --> 00:03:06,772
would lose much of their energy.

58
00:03:07,272 --> 00:03:11,491
And the strip of shore that floods and drains every day, home to crabs,

59
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:13,512
mussels and seaweed, would shrink.

60
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:20,088
Over the next few weeks, the strangest effects show up in nature.

61
00:03:20,088 --> 00:03:23,675
Because a huge number of living things keep time by the Moon.

62
00:03:24,175 --> 00:03:25,775
On the Great Barrier Reef,

63
00:03:25,775 --> 00:03:29,036
many corals release their eggs and sperm all at once,

64
00:03:29,036 --> 00:03:31,005
in the nights after a full moon.

65
00:03:31,005 --> 00:03:33,344
It looks like an underwater snowstorm.

66
00:03:33,844 --> 00:03:34,390
In 2007,

67
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:41,493
researchers found a light-sensing gene in corals that responds to the faint blue light of the full moon.

68
00:03:41,493 --> 00:03:44,840
Corals have no eyes, but they can sense the Moon.

69
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:48,571
Without moonlight, scientists think that cue is gone.

70
00:03:48,571 --> 00:03:52,474
And when spawning drifts out of sync, fewer eggs get fertilized.

71
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:55,870
On Christmas Island in the Indian Ocean,

72
00:03:55,870 --> 00:03:59,357
millions of red crabs march down to the sea every year.

73
00:03:59,857 --> 00:04:04,044
The females release their eggs before dawn, as the high tide turns,

74
00:04:04,044 --> 00:04:06,106
timed to the Moon's last quarter.

75
00:04:06,106 --> 00:04:09,168
Take away the Moon, and they lose their calendar.

76
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:11,106
In South Africa,

77
00:04:11,106 --> 00:04:20,229
one kind of dung beetle keeps its ball rolling in a straight line at night by reading the pattern of polarized moonlight across the sky.

78
00:04:20,229 --> 00:04:24,454
In 2003, it became the first animal known to navigate this way.

79
00:04:24,954 --> 00:04:27,961
And in the Arctic, during the long polar night,

80
00:04:27,961 --> 00:04:33,973
tiny animals called zooplankton swim up and down to the rhythm of the Moon instead of the Sun.

81
00:04:33,973 --> 00:04:37,683
Around each full moon, they sink, about fifty meters down,

82
00:04:37,683 --> 00:04:41,201
probably to hide from predators that hunt by moonlight.

83
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:46,573
Without the Moon, some of these creatures lose a compass, a clock or a hiding cue.

84
00:04:46,573 --> 00:04:47,998
Many would likely adapt.

85
00:04:47,998 --> 00:04:48,889
Some might not.

86
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:53,370
Quick favour before we go on.

87
00:04:53,370 --> 00:04:56,245
If you're enjoying this, tap like and subscribe.

88
00:04:56,245 --> 00:04:57,922
It really helps the channel.

89
00:04:58,422 --> 00:05:01,942
A year in, you'd notice what's missing from the calendar.

90
00:05:01,942 --> 00:05:02,992
No more eclipses.

91
00:05:02,992 --> 00:05:04,536
Not solar, and not lunar.

92
00:05:05,036 --> 00:05:08,174
Total solar eclipses are a cosmic coincidence.

93
00:05:08,174 --> 00:05:11,993
The Sun is about four hundred times wider than the Moon,

94
00:05:11,993 --> 00:05:15,198
but also about four hundred times farther away.

95
00:05:15,198 --> 00:05:18,881
So from Earth, they look almost exactly the same size.

96
00:05:19,381 --> 00:05:22,983
And those few minutes of darkness have changed science.

97
00:05:22,983 --> 00:05:24,686
During an eclipse in 1868,

98
00:05:24,686 --> 00:05:31,825
astronomers studying the Sun's glowing edge spotted a yellow line in its light that matched no known element.

99
00:05:31,825 --> 00:05:32,742
It was helium.

100
00:05:32,742 --> 00:05:36,409
Found on the Sun, years before anyone found it on Earth.

101
00:05:36,909 --> 00:05:37,457
In 1919,

102
00:05:37,457 --> 00:05:43,413
Arthur Eddington's team used a total eclipse to photograph stars right next to the Sun,

103
00:05:43,413 --> 00:05:46,972
and showed that the Sun's gravity bends their light,

104
00:05:46,972 --> 00:05:48,752
as Einstein had predicted.

105
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,533
Astronomers do get one upside.

106
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:56,881
Dark skies, every night of the month, with no full moon washing out faint galaxies.

107
00:05:57,381 --> 00:05:59,812
And the month itself loses its meaning.

108
00:05:59,812 --> 00:06:02,992
It began as the time from one new moon to the next,

109
00:06:02,992 --> 00:06:05,112
about twenty-nine and a half days.

110
00:06:05,112 --> 00:06:09,476
Calendars still built on the Moon's phases, like the Islamic calendar,

111
00:06:09,476 --> 00:06:11,533
would have nothing left to count.

112
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:15,464
Now jump far ahead.

113
00:06:15,464 --> 00:06:18,995
The biggest question about a moonless Earth is its tilt.

114
00:06:19,495 --> 00:06:24,547
Earth's axis leans about twenty-three degrees, and that tilt gives you seasons.

115
00:06:24,547 --> 00:06:29,023
Today it only rocks gently, by a little more than a degree either way,

116
00:06:29,023 --> 00:06:31,070
over about forty thousand years.

117
00:06:31,570 --> 00:06:32,115
In 1993,

118
00:06:32,115 --> 00:06:37,908
French astronomer Jacques Laskar and his colleagues ran the numbers without the Moon.

119
00:06:37,908 --> 00:06:39,680
Their answer was alarming.

120
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:43,702
Over long spans of time, the tilt could wander chaotically,

121
00:06:43,702 --> 00:06:47,246
anywhere from zero to more than eighty-five degrees.

122
00:06:47,746 --> 00:06:50,098
Mars shows what that might look like.

123
00:06:50,098 --> 00:06:54,230
It has no big moon, and its tilt is thought to swing chaotically,

124
00:06:54,230 --> 00:06:56,645
from below ten degrees to above sixty.

125
00:06:56,645 --> 00:07:01,540
Tip a planet far enough, and its poles can face the Sun for months at a time.

126
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:03,096
But wait.

127
00:07:03,096 --> 00:07:08,357
In 2011, a team including Jason Barnes and Jack Lissauer ran longer,

128
00:07:08,357 --> 00:07:11,916
more detailed simulations of a moonless Earth.

129
00:07:12,416 --> 00:07:15,500
They found that over hundreds of millions of years,

130
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:19,007
the tilt might wander by only about ten to twenty degrees.

131
00:07:19,007 --> 00:07:23,844
That's a much bigger swing than today, but nowhere near the chaos people feared.

132
00:07:24,344 --> 00:07:26,633
So the Moon helps keep our seasons steady.

133
00:07:26,633 --> 00:07:30,066
But a moonless Earth might not have been as wild as we thought.

134
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:33,328
One thing would change for certain.

135
00:07:33,328 --> 00:07:36,797
The Moon's tides act like a brake on Earth's spin.

136
00:07:36,797 --> 00:07:40,683
Every century, they add a tiny bit of length to the day.

137
00:07:40,683 --> 00:07:45,609
Records of ancient eclipses suggest about 1.8 milliseconds per century.

138
00:07:46,109 --> 00:07:47,697
That sounds like nothing.

139
00:07:47,697 --> 00:07:48,650
But it adds up.

140
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:51,571
A fossil shell from seventy million years ago,

141
00:07:51,571 --> 00:07:53,477
from the age of the dinosaurs,

142
00:07:53,477 --> 00:07:57,287
recorded days only about twenty-three and a half hours long.

143
00:07:57,287 --> 00:08:00,336
Its year had three hundred and seventy-two days.

144
00:08:00,836 --> 00:08:05,032
Without the Moon, the Sun's weaker tides would still brake our spin,

145
00:08:05,032 --> 00:08:06,266
but far more gently.

146
00:08:06,266 --> 00:08:10,585
Your days would stay close to twenty-four hours for much, much longer.

147
00:08:11,433 --> 00:08:12,638
And here's the twist.

148
00:08:12,638 --> 00:08:14,245
The Moon is already leaving.

149
00:08:14,745 --> 00:08:18,012
Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon's surface.

150
00:08:18,012 --> 00:08:21,648
Observatories still fire lasers at them, and time the echo,

151
00:08:21,648 --> 00:08:24,976
which takes about two and a half seconds to come back.

152
00:08:25,476 --> 00:08:26,264
The answer:

153
00:08:26,264 --> 00:08:30,418
the Moon is drifting away by about 3.8 centimeters a year.

154
00:08:30,418 --> 00:08:33,355
Roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

155
00:08:33,355 --> 00:08:35,288
It's that same tidal brake.

156
00:08:35,288 --> 00:08:39,657
As Earth's spin slows, the Moon is nudged into a wider orbit.

157
00:08:40,157 --> 00:08:42,476
So it will slowly shrink in our sky.

158
00:08:42,476 --> 00:08:51,362
NASA lunar scientist Richard Vondrak estimated that Earth's last total solar eclipse will happen about six hundred million years from now.

159
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:53,451
But it won't escape.

160
00:08:53,451 --> 00:08:55,201
In about five billion years,

161
00:08:55,201 --> 00:08:58,078
the Sun is expected to swell into a red giant,

162
00:08:58,078 --> 00:09:03,768
and scientists expect that to wreck the Earth and Moon long before the Moon could get away.

163
00:09:04,268 --> 00:09:06,484
And it all began just as violently.

164
00:09:06,484 --> 00:09:11,105
Scientists think the Moon formed about four and a half billion years ago,

165
00:09:11,105 --> 00:09:15,600
when a Mars-sized world, nicknamed Theia, smashed into the young Earth.

166
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:20,411
One simulation suggests the Moon could have taken shape in just a few hours.

167
00:09:21,267 --> 00:09:23,955
So the Moon isn't just a light in the sky.

168
00:09:23,955 --> 00:09:28,307
It's a tide maker, a clock for corals and crabs, an eclipse machine,

169
00:09:28,307 --> 00:09:29,843
and a brake on our spin.

170
00:09:29,843 --> 00:09:32,788
And it's leaving us, one fingernail at a time.

171
00:09:33,288 --> 00:09:35,935
If it vanished tonight, what would you miss most?

172
00:09:35,935 --> 00:09:38,205
The tides, the eclipses, or the moonlight?

173
00:09:38,205 --> 00:09:39,502
Tell me in the comments.
